## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates combined data structures and time-based eviction. Tests ability to design per-key streaming aggregates with real-time top-K, using arrays/linked lists/hashtable/heap and lazy deletions.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window aggregation; - Hash maps for per-tag buckets; - Deques for per-tag event queues; - Max-heap with lazy invalidation for top-K.

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingTagTopK {
  constructor(windowMs){ this.W=windowMs; this.tagMap=new Map(); this.heap=[]; this.ver=0; }
  _expire(tag, now){ const b=this.tagMap.get(tag); while (b.queue.length && b.queue[0].ts <= now - this.W){ b.sum -= b.queue.shift().val; } }
  addEvent(tag, userId, value, ts){ let b=this.tagMap.get(tag); if(!b){ b={sum:0, queue:[], ver:0}; this.tagMap.set(tag,b);} this._expire(tag, ts); b.queue.push({ts, val: value}); b.sum += value; this.ver++; // push new entry
    this._heapPush({sum: b.sum, tag, ver:this.ver}); }
  topK(K){ // lazy
    const res=[]; const seen=new Set(); while (res.length<K && this.heap.length){ const top=this._heapPop(); if (top.ver===this.tagMap.get(top.tag).ver){ res.push(top.tag); seen.add(top.tag); } }
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle deletions or varying window sizes per tag?
- How would you optimize memory and support concurrent producers?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic dictionary updates with a prefix-based query, combining trie augmentation and edge-case handling. 

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node passCount to track subtree content
- O(length of word) updates for insert/delete
- LCP query via prefix traversal until no valid child
- Edge cases: duplicates, deleting absent words, memory pruning

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.cnt = 0;
  }
}
class DynamicDict {
  constructor(){ this.root = new TrieNode(); this.hasWord=new Set(); }
  insert(w){ if(this.hasWord.has(w)) return; this.hasWord.add(w); let node=this.root; for(const ch of w){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch,new TrieNode()); node=node.next.get(ch); node.cnt++; } }
  delete(w){ if(!this.hasWord.has(w)) return; this.hasWord.delete(w); let node=this.root; for(const ch of w){ const child=node.next.get(ch); child.cnt--; if(child.cnt===0){ node.next.delete(ch); } node=child; } }
  lcpQuery(q){ let node=this.root, depth=0, res=0; for(const ch of q){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) break; node=node.next.get(ch); depth++; if(node.cnt>0) res=depth; } return res; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you support Unicode or a larger alphabet efficiently? 
- Compare memory trade-offs with an augmented BST of words versus the Trie approach.

## Purpose
This problem tests sliding-window aggregation with per-key state management, lazy expiration strategies, and top-K extraction using a heap. It integrates multiple data structures: hashtables for O(1) key lookups, per-key queues for temporal ordering, and a heap for maintaining ranked sums.

## Key Concepts
- **Sliding window maintenance**: Events expire once they exceed the window width W
- **Per-key accumulation**: Each key maintains its own queue of (timestamp, value) pairs
- **Lazy eviction**: Stale entries are removed only when encountered, avoiding unnecessary cleanup
- **Amortized efficiency**: The cost of expiration is distributed across active operations

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowedTopKMap {
  constructor(windowWidth) {
    this.W = windowWidth;
    this.map = new Map();
    this.heap = new MinHeap();
  }

  addEvent(key, value, ts) {
    if (!this.map.has(key)) {
      this.map.set(key, { sum: 0, queue: [] });
    }
    const entry = this.map.get(key);
    entry.queue.push({ ts, value });
    entry.sum += value;
    this.heap.push({ sum: entry.sum, key });
  }

  advanceTime(now) {
    // Lazy cleanup handled in topKKeys
  }

  topKKeys(K, now) {
    const results = [];
    while (results.length < K && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const top = this.heap.pop();
      const entry = this.map.get(top.key);
      if (!entry) continue;
      this._expire(entry.queue, now);
      if (Math.abs(entry.sum - top.sum) > 0.0001) continue;
      results.push(top.key);
    }
    return results;
  }

  _expire(queue, now) {
    while (queue.length && queue[0].ts <= now - this.W) {
      entry.sum -= queue.shift().value;
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle negative values, where sum could decrease over time?
- Could you support variable window sizes per key if needed?
- What memory bounds exist, and how could you proactively prune empty keys?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of introsort-style hybrids, balancing average performance with worst-case guarantees in production environments. It tests pivot strategies, partitioning, depth tracking, and in-place constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: hybrid of quicksort and heapsort with depth-based switch
- Median-of-three pivot: reduces chance of worst-case partitions
- 3-way partitioning: handles duplicates efficiently
- Insertion sort for tiny subarrays: amortized gains

## Code Example

```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(Math.log2(arr.length)) * 2;
  function insertionSort(a, lo, hi) {
    for (let i = lo + 1; i < hi; i++) {
      let key = a[i], j = i - 1;
      while (j >= lo && a[j] > key) { a[j + 1] = a[j]; j--; }
      a[j + 1] = key;
    }
  }
  function heapify(a, count, i, offset) {
    // placeholder for in-place heapify within [offset, offset+count)
  }
  function partition(a, lo, hi) {
    // median-of-three and 3-way partitioning placeholder
    return Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
  }
  function sort(a, lo, hi, depth) {
    if (hi - lo <= 16) { insertionSort(a, lo, hi); return; }
    if (depth > maxDepth) { /* heapsort(a, lo, hi); */ return; }
    const pivot = partition(a, lo, hi);
    // recursively sort partitions
    sort(a, lo, pivot, depth + 1);
    sort(a, pivot, hi, depth + 1);
  }
  sort(arr, 0, arr.length, 0);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to be stable, or explain why stability is costly in-place?
- Compare this approach with TimSort or other stable hybrids in real-world libraries.
- What are the practical pitfalls when implementing pivot selection and depth bounds in a multi-threaded setting?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about worst-case guarantees and practical trade-offs between quicksort and mergesort, including depth control, partition balance, and memory usage in a real-world data processing pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid sorting (introspective sort)
- Worst-case guarantees, space complexity
- Handling duplicates, stability considerations
- Practical performance on sorted/partially sorted data

## Code Example
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>

// Simplified hybrid sort: quicksort with depth limit, switch to mergesort when needed
int partition(std::vector<long long>& a, int l, int r) {
  long long pivot = a[(l + r) / 2];
  int i = l, j = r;
  while (i <= j) {
    while (a[i] < pivot) ++i;
    while (a[j] > pivot) --j;
    if (i <= j) std::swap(a[i++], a[j--]);
  }
  return i;
}

void mergeSort(std::vector<long long>& a, int l, int r, std::vector<long long>& tmp) {
  if (r - l <= 1) return;
  int m = (l + r) / 2;
  mergeSort(a, l, m, tmp);
  mergeSort(a, m, r, tmp);
  int i = l, j = m, k = 0;
  while (i < m || j < r) {
    if (j >= r || (i < m && a[i] <= a[j])) tmp[k++] = a[i++];
    else tmp[k++] = a[j++];
  }
  for (int t = 0; t < k; ++t) a[l + t] = tmp[t];
}

void hybridSort(std::vector<long long>& a, int l, int r, int depth, int depthLimit) {
  if (r - l <= 16) {
    std::sort(a.begin() + l, a.begin() + r + 1);
    return;
  }
  if (depth >= depthLimit) {
    std::vector<long long> tmp(r - l + 1);
    mergeSort(a, l, r + 1, tmp);
    return;
  }
  int p = partition(a, l, r);
  hybridSort(a, l, p - 1, depth + 1, depthLimit);
  hybridSort(a, p, r, depth + 1, depthLimit);
}

// Entry: call with depth=0 and depthLimit=2*log2(n)
```
